  "summary": "The South San Francisco City Council expressed preliminary support for installing 25 automated license plate readers near Highway 101 and I-380 on/off-ramps to deter crime, particularly smash-and-grab break-ins along the hotel corridor. Police Capt. Scott Campbell described data retention (30 days local, then 1 year in a Northern California database), access policies barring ICE under state sanctuary law, and councilmembers raised questions about privacy, breaches, and data sharing.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The data is stored on encrypted servers for 30 days before being transferred to a Northern California database where it is stored for one year before being deleted, unless the vehicle is suspected of being involved in a crime",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "per county and state rules, Immigration and Customs Enforcement would be strictly barred",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "Police in Pasadena and Long Beach were found to have shared ALPR captured data with ICE last year, despite the state's sanctuary law",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    }
  ],
